course: dictionary
lesson: 1869
title: One word, many meanings: cold
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word cold can mean different things. «Cold water» means low temperature. «I'm cold» means your body does not feel warm enough. «A cold look» is about feelings, not temperature. To choose the right meaning, read the words around it.
explain: Look at the clues: «That is why your hot cocoa cools on the table and an ice cube melts in your warm hand, and why your hand feels cold: it is losing its heat to the ice.» Heat, melts and warm tell you cold is about temperature, and your hand is losing heat.
explain: Ask: what word would fit here? Try your meaning in the sentence. In «A cold look», low temperature makes no sense, so cold must mean not friendly or kind.
fact: Cold can be an adjective: «cold water», «colder», «coldest».
fact: Cold can be a noun: «the cold» means cold weather, and «a cold» is an illness with a runny nose, sneezes and a sore throat.
fact: «Ice-cold» means very cold, and «out in the cold» means left out of things.
fact: Water freezes at 0 °C, which is cold enough to turn into ice.
fact: A cold is caused by germs called viruses, and washing your hands helps stop them spreading.
exercise: «Wet skin on very cold metal freezes and sticks to it.» What does cold mean here? | *Very low temperature | Having an illness | Not friendly | Sad and tired
exercise: Sam says, «I'm cold, can I have my jumper?» What is Sam telling you? | Sam is angry with you | Sam wants a cold drink | Sam has a runny nose | *Sam's body does not feel warm enough
exercise: «He gave her a cold look and turned away.» What does cold suggest? | Icy weather | A sore throat | Low temperature | *Not friendly or kind
test: «The cold weather means we must dress warmly.» What does the cold mean here? | A friendly greeting | A frozen lake | *Cold weather | A runny nose
test: Ben sneezes all day and says, «I have a cold.» What is a cold? | A kind of ice cream | A cold drink of water | *An illness with a runny nose and sneezes | A friendly greeting
exam: Which sentence uses cold to mean low temperature? | «Sam was out in the cold, left out of the game.» | «Her cold look made me shiver.» | *«The ice-cold water made me gasp.» | «He gave a cold reply.»
